---
import BlogPostLayout from '../../layouts/BlogPostLayout.astro';

export const frontmatter = {
  title: 'The Activation-Function Ranking Depends On Your Block, Not Your Data',
  pubDate: '2026-07-03',
};
---

<BlogPostLayout frontmatter={frontmatter}>
  <p class="mb-6 text-gray-700">I set out to run a small benchmark to pick an activation function for a tiny transformer I was building — expecting to confirm the folk knowledge everyone teaches: tanh saturates, ReLU dies, GELU is the modern default, SiLU is a slightly better ReLU, and Mish is a slightly better SiLU. Three hundred and twelve runs later, that story has holes in it. This post walks through what I actually saw across eight activation functions and five tasks, and focuses on the finding that changed how I think about the choice: <b>which activation wins doesn't depend on your data, it depends on your block.</b></p>

  <div class="mb-6 p-4 rounded border bg-blue-50 text-blue-900">
    <b>TL;DR.</b> <b>SwiGLU wins every transformer task tested</b> — WikiText-2 LM by 11.6 PPL, WikiText-103 by 1.55 PPL, CIFAR-10 ViT by +2.5% top-1 (all with Cohen's <i>d</i> ≥ 2, most with <i>p</i> &lt; 10<sup>-4</sup>). <b>GELU is universal #2.</b> But two textbook claims fail. First, "tanh saturates" doesn't hold at this scale — only 0.4% of post-activations saturate at the end of training. When I tried the natural fix and multiplied tanh by 4 to unbound its range, it got <i>worse</i> by 12 PPL, not better. Second, on the same CIFAR-10 dataset a ResNet+BN prefers SiLU (85.7% top-1) while a ViT-tiny prefers SwiGLU/GELU (77.1%). The block, not the modality, drives the ranking — and the underlying probe that predicts rank flips sign between them.
  </div>

  <nav class="mb-6 p-4 rounded border bg-gray-50 text-gray-700 text-sm md:text-base">
    <b class="block mb-1">Contents</b>
    <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-0.5">
      <li><a href="#setup" class="text-blue-700 hover:underline">The setup — 8 × 5 × 8 = 312 runs</a></li>
      <li><a href="#headline" class="text-blue-700 hover:underline">The headline — one heatmap, five surprises</a></li>
      <li><a href="#swiglu" class="text-blue-700 hover:underline">Finding 1 — SwiGLU wins every transformer task</a></li>
      <li><a href="#gelu" class="text-blue-700 hover:underline">Finding 2 — GELU is the universal #2</a></li>
      <li><a href="#block-not-modality" class="text-blue-700 hover:underline">Finding 3 — The block drives the ranking, not the modality</a></li>
      <li><a href="#leaky" class="text-blue-700 hover:underline">Finding 4 — LeakyReLU with slope 0.01 does nothing</a></li>
